<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kara Mustafa Pasha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1634–<a href="/years/1683">1683</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kara Mustafa Pasha was an Ottoman grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of the late seventeenth century. He led the Ottoman siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1683, which was broken by a relief army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-iii-sobieski">Jan III Sobieski</a>. The defeat led to his execution and began a long series of Ottoman territorial losses in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kara-mustafa-pasha.jpg" alt="Kara Mustafa Pasha" width="317" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rijksmuseum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portret_van_Kara_Mustafa_Pasha,_RP-P-1894-A-18289.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kara Mustafa Pasha was of Turkish origin, the son of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sipahi">sipahi</a>, a cavalryman, and was born in the village of Mirince near Merzifon. He was brought up in the household of the Köprülü family, who were of Albanian origin, and was educated by Köprülü Mehmed Pasha and married into the family. Within the household he served as letter-carrier to the grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and as armourer, and then entered the sultan's household as master of the horse. European observers described him as greedy and intransigent, though some, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> agent Colyer, found him agreeable at first. He also made many enemies in the Ottoman army and palace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1659 he became governor of Silistria. Within ten years he was acting as deputy for his brother-in-law, the grand vizier Köprülüzade Fazıl Ahmed Pasha, when the latter was absent from court. As commander of ground troops in a war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> he negotiated a settlement with John Sobieski in 1676 that added the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a> to the empire. When Fazıl Ahmed Pasha died in the same year, Kara Mustafa succeeded him as grand vizier.</p><p>He then led campaigns against the Tsardom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to support the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> state, an Ottoman vassal, and placed Ottoman garrisons in many cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. He took the fortress of Chigirin and fought Grigory Romodanovsky on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> between 14 and 20 August. Neither commander won decisively, and logistical difficulties on the steppe forced Mustafa to withdraw. After the battle he took the fortress of Kaniv by siege.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1683 Kara Mustafa marched north into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, on his own initiative and without the knowledge of Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-iv">Mehmed IV</a>; his army lacked heavy siege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>. By mid-July about 100,000 men were besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, which was held by some 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> soldiers, and by September he had taken part of the walls. On 12 September 1683 a Polish army under King John III Sobieski won the Battle of Vienna with a flank attack by its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-hussars">Winged Hussars</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> retreated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, much of which was later conquered by the Habsburgs and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1684">Holy League</a> allies.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-iv">Mehmed IV</a> ordered him executed, and on 25 December 1683 he was strangled with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> cord at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>. Historians describe him as either a capable tactician or a reckless commander. His birthplace was renamed Karamustafapaşa in his honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kara Mustafa Pasha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The second siege of Vienna failed in September 1683, when a relief army of imperial German and Polish troops under Jan III Sobieski routed the forces of Grand Vizier <b>Kara Mustafa Pasha</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In war, the Commonwealth was decisive in the relief of Vienna in 1683, where Jan III Sobieski led the great cavalry charge that broke <b>Kara Mustafa</b>’s besieging army, and it joined the Holy League that went on to roll back Ottoman power in central Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Philip V brought French methods to Spain, Peter the Great’s Russia pursued mercantilism, and the Ottoman Grand Vizier Kemankeş <b>Kara Mustafa Pasha</b> followed some of its financial policies.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/mercantilism">Mercantilism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Merzifon, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.87,35.46&amp;year=1665" aria-label="Merzifon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5418 705 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kara Mustafa Pasha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5868 975h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5868 975h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.87,35.46&amp;year=1665">What was here in 1665? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
